  "response": "I’m using the repository’s `crimes-codebase-risk` skill because this is a scanner-driven TypeScript refactor review. I’ll verify the scoped findings against the three source files, then group a concrete, type-tightening plan by finding type without changing code.\n\nThe skill locator in the session metadata isn’t present at the advertised relative path, so I’m locating the checked-in copy before proceeding. The review itself remains read-only.\n\nThe skill’s decision rule is to plan from JSON evidence and prioritize by agent risk. I’m now rerunning the scoped scan locally and reading the exact functions so the proposal distinguishes real public contracts from nested callback shapes that merely look like one function returning six unrelated objects.\n\nThe rerun matches the supplied 92-finding report. One important nuance from the source: the Svelte “roulette” is largely the scanner collapsing several independently typed esbuild/Svelte callbacks into one apparent return contract; the refactor should expose those callback boundaries, not invent one artificial mega-union. I’m checking the existing option and dependency types now so the proposed signatures are implementable.\n\nThe scan reproduces 92 total findings. The requested cluster contains eight findings across three types; no code was changed.\n\n| Finding type | Scoped findings |\n|---|---|\n| `name_behavior_mismatch` | `crime_00004` `getRollupConfig`; `crime_00013` `buildStart`; `crime_00022` `build`; `crime_00023` `buildAll` |\n| `option_bag_junk_drawer` | `crime_00008` `getRollupConfig` |\n| `return_shape_roulette` | `crime_00019` `sveltePlugin`; `crime_00020` `setup`; `crime_00021` anonymous `onLoad` callback |\n\nThe same functions also trigger large-function findings, so extracting the type and naming boundaries below should reduce those without requiring an unrelated redesign.\n\n## `name_behavior_mismatch`\n\nIn [src/rollup.ts](</private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-andrew-dev-crimes/ad0f3f9a-0962-407f-b4e5-9d994a4a18e5/scratchpad/wt-eval/evals/fixtures/04-monorepo/src/rollup.ts:38>), `getRollupConfig` sounds like a read-only accessor, but it changes global logging through `setSilent`, reads package/dependency information, mutates the nested `options.dts` object, and installs a plugin that later deletes files.\n\nRefactor it into:\n\n- `resolveDtsRollupContext(...)`: package/dependency and compiler-option resolution.\n- `createDtsRollupConfig(...)`: pure configuration construction.\n- Move `setSilent` into `startRollup`, where process-level effects are expected.\n- Construct `dtsOptions` immutably instead of assigning `dtsOptions.entry`.\n- If the operations remain combined, call it `prepareDtsRollupConfig`, not `getRollupConfig`.\n\nAn agent could currently memoize or call the apparent getter during inspection, unknowingly alter logger state or shared options, and assume repeated calls are harmless.\n\n`buildStart` is different: it is a Rollup lifecycle hook whose name is fixed by Rollup. Renaming it would silently disable declaration cleanup. Extract the behavior instead:\n\n```ts\nconst cleanDeclarationOutputs = async (...): Promise<void> => { ... }\n\nconst cleanPlugin: Plugin = {\n  name: \"tsup:clean\",\n  buildStart: cleanDeclarationOutputs,\n}\n```\n\nThis specific finding is a framework-hook false positive. If it remains after extraction, record:\n\n```bash\ncrimes feedback 'name_behavior_mismatch::src/rollup.ts::buildStart' \\\n  --verdict fp \\\n  --note \"buildStart is a Rollup lifecycle hook whose API-defined purpose is to perform side effects.\"\n```\n\nIn [src/index.ts](</private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-andrew-dev-crimes/ad0f3f9a-0962-407f-b4e5-9d994a4a18e5/scratchpad/wt-eval/evals/fixtures/04-monorepo/src/index.ts:167>), preserve the public `build` export for compatibility, but make it a thin, explicitly imperative façade:\n\n```ts\nexport async function build(options: Options): Promise<void> {\n  return executeBuildInvocation(options)\n}\n```\n\nThen rename/extract the internals:\n\n- Anonymous per-config callback → `executeBuildConfig`.\n- `mainTasks` → `runBundlerPipeline`.\n- `buildAll` → `runBuildCycle` or `rebuildBundlerOutputs`.\n- `startWatcher` → `registerBuildWatcher`.\n- Extract `cleanOutput`, `buildAllFormats`, `copyPublicAssets`, and `runSuccessHook`.\n\n`buildAll` is particularly misleading because it does not perform the separate declaration task, but it does clean files, build every JS format, copy assets, and execute `onSuccess`. An agent could either duplicate declaration work or omit it while assuming “all” really means all.\n\n## `option_bag_junk_drawer`\n\n`getRollupConfig` accepts the entire `NormalizedOptions` contract while owning only ten fields:\n\n```ts\ntype DtsRollupOptions = Readonly<\n  Pick<\n    NormalizedOptions,\n    | \"cjsInterop\"\n    | \"clean\"\n    | \"dts\"\n    | \"entry\"\n    | \"external\"\n    | \"format\"\n    | \"outDir\"\n    | \"outExtension\"\n    | \"silent\"\n    | \"tsconfig\"\n  >\n>\n```\n\nUse that narrow type at the orchestration boundary, then split it further for helpers:\n\n- Input: `entry`, `dts`, `external`, `tsconfig`.\n- Output: `format`, `outDir`, `outExtension`, `cjsInterop`.\n- Runtime/cleanup: `clean`, `silent`.\n\nPrefer readonly inputs and fresh objects for `dts` and output arrays. Rename `RollupConfig` to `DtsRollupPlan` to distinguish the generated Rollup configuration from user-facing tsup options.\n\nToday an agent can easily:\n\n- Pass raw `Options` before normalization and assume `format`, `entry`, or `outDir` are present.\n- Add another unrelated property read because the whole global options object is available.\n- Mutate `options.dts` without realizing other build stages may observe the same object.\n- Confuse configuration construction with runtime behavior.\n\n## `return_shape_roulette`\n\nThe three findings in [src/esbuild/svelte.ts](</private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-andrew-dev-crimes/ad0f3f9a-0962-407f-b4e5-9d994a4a18e5/scratchpad/wt-eval/evals/fixtures/04-monorepo/src/esbuild/svelte.ts:9>) need careful interpretation: `sveltePlugin` already has the explicit return type `Plugin`. The six object shapes belong to several different nested contracts:\n\n- esbuild resolver: `{ path, namespace }`\n- esbuild loader success: `{ contents, warnings }`\n- esbuild loader failure: `{ errors }`\n- esbuild diagnostic: `{ text, location }`\n- Svelte preprocessing: `{ code }` or `{ code, map }`\n\nDo not combine these into one mega-union. Extract and type each boundary:\n\n```ts\nresolveSvelteCss(args: OnResolveArgs): OnResolveResult\ntoEsbuildMessage(diagnostic: SvelteDiagnostic, source: SourceContext): PartialMessage\npreprocessTypeScript(...): Promise<{ code: string; map?: string }>\nloadSvelteComponent(...): Promise<SvelteLoadResult>\nsetupSveltePlugin(build: PluginBuild, options: SveltePluginOptions): void\n```\n\nMake `SvelteLoadResult` stricter than esbuild’s permissive type:\n\n```ts\ntype SvelteLoadResult =\n  | { contents: string; warnings: PartialMessage[]; errors?: never }\n  | { errors: PartialMessage[]; contents?: never }\n```\n\nThis prevents an agent from returning both output and errors—or neither—while remaining assignable to esbuild’s `OnLoadResult`.\n\nCurrently, an agent working inside the 91-line anonymous callback can confuse `{ code }` with esbuild’s required `{ contents }`, use a Svelte diagnostic where an `OnLoadResult` is expected, or change one error branch without noticing the other result contracts.\n\n## Suggested implementation order\n\n1. Add behavior tests for invalid Svelte syntax and diagnostic locations; existing tests already cover normal CSS, no-CSS, TypeScript, and Sass paths.\n2. Extract and explicitly type the Svelte callbacks.\n3. Narrow the Rollup inputs, stop mutating `options.dts`, and separate context resolution from plan construction.\n4. Turn public `build` into a stable façade and extract the named build-cycle operations.\n5. Run the project typecheck/build/tests, then rescan these fingerprints with JSON output. Any new high-severity finding is a blocker; `buildStart` may remain as the documented Rollup-hook false positive.",
